# Build output
# dist/ is COMMITTED so git+ssh consumers (no build step) get pre-built JS/d.ts.
# Run `npm run build` after editing src/, then commit the regenerated dist/.
*.tsbuildinfo

# Dependencies
node_modules/

# Lock files
# package-lock.json IS committed, reversing the original choice recorded here.
# That choice was "not committed for a library — consumers maintain their own
# lock file; this also avoids leaking host-specific registry URLs."
#
# Both halves were sound in isolation, and both are answered:
#   - Consumers really do maintain their own lock file (npm ignores a
#     dependency's lock entirely), but that argues committing one does not HELP
#     consumers — not that omitting it is free. It costs us: because dist/ is
#     committed (see above), the build inputs must be pinned or that artifact is
#     only reproducible against whatever npm resolved on the day. This is not
#     hypothetical — a floating @types/react rewrote every JSX.Element in
#     dist/*.d.ts to React.JSX.Element between two builds of identical source.
#   - Registry-URL leaking is already handled by .npmrc pinning the public
#     registry; every resolved URL in the lock file is registry.npmjs.org.
# `npm ci` also needs the lock file, and CI should use it to build dist/.
#
# Other package managers' locks stay ignored — one authoritative lock only.
pnpm-lock.yaml
yarn.lock

# Logs / temp
*.log
.turbo/
.DS_Store

# Editor
.vscode/
.idea/
